<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-simple-algebras">
    <title>Exercises related to simple algebras</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Most of the exercises are taken from <xref ref="bib-Pierce"/>. We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity, and <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
        </p>
    </introduction>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            For an <m>R</m>-algebra <m>A</m> and an <m>A</m>-submodule <m>M\leq A_A</m> show the following.
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>B=\{a\in A:aM\subseteq M\}</m> then, <m>B</m> is an <m>A</m>-subalgebra such that <m>M</m> is a two-sided ideal of <m>B</m>. Furthermore, <m>\End{A}{A_A/M}\simeq B/M</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>M=eA</m> for an idempotent <m>e\in A</m> then, <m>\End{A}{M}\simeq eAe</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Suppose that <m>A=M_n(D)</m> for a division algebra <m>D</m> and that <m>N_i=E_{ii}A</m>. Using the above part, show that <m>\End{A}{N_i}\simeq D</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A nonzero idempotent <m>e\in A</m> is said to be <term>primitive</term> if <m>e</m> can not be written as <m>e=e_1+e_2</m> with <m>0\neq e_i</m>, <m>e_i^2=e_i</m>, and <m>e_ie_j=0=e_je_i</m>. For a semisimple algebra show that following statements are equivalent of an idempotent <m>e\in A</m>:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>e</m> is primitive;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>eA</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>eAe</m> is a division algebra.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The converse of Schur's lemma is not true in general as illustrated below.
        </p>
        <p>
            Let <m>F</m> be a field and consider <m>A=F\oplus F\oplus F</m>. Define a bilinear multiplication <m>A\times A\to A</m> by
            <me>
                (a_1,a_2,a_3)(b_1,b_2,b_3)\mapsto(a_1b_1,a_2b_2,a_1b_3+a_3b_2).
            </me>
            Show that
            <ol>
                            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>A</m> is an <m>F</m>-algebra;
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>e=(1,0,0)</m> is an idempotent;
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>eA</m> is not simple;
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>End{A}{eA}\simeq F</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</worksheet>